> Derive the capped window and the jitter law

For base b>=0, cap c>=0 and k>=0, define

\[
W_k=\min(b\,2^k,c),\qquad D_k=u_k W_k.
\]

If b>=c, the cap applies immediately. If b=0, every window is zero.
For 0<b<c, doubling reaches the cap at the first k with b·2^k>=c.
This exponential notation is mathematical; calculating 2^k directly or
forming an overflowing product is unnecessary.

The core decomposes b and c into base-two mantissas and exponents with
frexp. Comparing k with their widened exponent gap, and the mantissas
when those exponents align, decides whether the cap branch applies.
Otherwise ldexp scales b by 2^k within the finite range below c.
This uses a fixed number of scalar math operations even for INT_MAX.
The emitted step records say whether the cap or actual ldexp branch ran;
they do not count CPU instructions or promise a real-time deadline.

For an ideal independent U uniformly distributed on [0,1), full jitter
D=UW has density 1/W over a positive window, so

\[
E[D]=\int_0^W\frac{d}{W}\,\mathrm{d}d=\frac W2,
\qquad \operatorname{Var}(D)=\frac{W^2}{12}.
\]

The zero-window case is a point mass at zero. Finite pseudorandom
variates and floating products are discrete approximations; the supplied
u=1 endpoint is a deterministic control, not a draw from that ideal law.
Native output always stays within the closed interval [0,W].

With independent ideal draws and B equal-width timer bins covering
[0,W], two clients share a bin with probability 1/B. Among C clients,
the expected number of colliding unordered pairs is C(C−1)/(2B).
This remains positive: jitter spreads load, and timer quantisation can
still produce collisions. No-jitter clients starting together share the
same bin with probability one. These assumptions are not a claim of
independence for arbitrary user seeds or of improved service latency.

> Retain the original base-one, cap-thirty example

The original eight windows and jitter values are reproduced by a native
seeded schedule. Its stream is xorshift64 with shifts 13,7,17, not the
LCG named in the old comment. Each actual state transition supplies the
high 53 bits times 2^-53. The default nonzero seed is the original
88172645463325252. The generator is deterministic and noncryptographic;
reusing a seed repeats the entire schedule.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/backoff_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");const auto windows=dm::algorithms::exponential_backoff_sequence(8,1,30);const auto s=dm::algorithms::exponential_backoff_seeded(8,1,30);std::vector<double>x,delays;
  std::print("| original k | capped window | actual unit variate | original jittered wait | cumulative idle wait |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<s.steps.size();++i){const auto& r=s.steps[i];if(r.window!=windows[i]||r.delay!=dm::algorithms::exponential_backoff_jittered(r.attempt,1,30,r.u))throw std::runtime_error("original scalar/schedule mismatch");std::print("| {} | {:.0f} | {:.9f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n",i,r.window,r.u,r.delay,s.retry_times[i]);x.push_back(i);delays.push_back(r.delay);}
  std::print("\nOriginal windows {}; generated steps {}; jittered total idle wait {:.6f}.\n",windows,s.steps.size(),s.total_delay);
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();plot.line(x,windows,"Capped maximum",{37,99,235},2).points(x,windows,"",{37,99,235},4).points(x,delays,"Actual original jitter",{220,38,38},5)
    .title("Original eight waits: each draw stays within its window").x_label("Zero-based wait index k").y_label("Window or delay (caller time units)").y_limits(0,33).size(1000,530).save_svg("build/backoff_original.svg");
}
